<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jamal al-Din al-Afghani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1838/39–<a href="/years/1897">1897</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Jamal ad-Din al-Afghani, Jamal ad-Din Asadabadi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jamal al-Din al-Afghani was a political activist and Islamic thinker who travelled across the Muslim world in the late 19th century, one of the founders of Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modernism">modernism</a>. He claimed Afghan origin, but several scholars hold that he was born into a Shia family in Asadabad, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> he gained a disciple, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abduh">Muhammad Abduh</a>, with whom he published the journal al-'Urwa al-wuthqa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1884, calling for unity among Muslims. He was implicated in the plot of his follower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirza-reza-kermani">Mirza Reza Kermani</a>, who shot Naser al-Din Shah of Iran in 1896.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jamal-al-din-al-afghani.jpg" alt="Jamal al-Din al-Afghani" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al_afghani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>His name al-Afghani claims an Afghan origin, and by his own account he was born in Asadabad near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>. Nikki Keddie and several other scholars hold that he was raised in a Shia family in Asadabad near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and that he claimed Afghan birth to win influence among Sunni Muslims or to escape the Shah. His rivals Shaykh Yusuf al-Nabhani and Shaykh Abu al-Huda al-Sayyadi called him Mutaʾafghin, meaning the one who claims to be Afghan. He was educated at home, then in Qazvin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> and the Twelver shrine cities of Ottoman Iraq.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1856–57 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> and spent some years studying religions. In 1866 he is recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, where he advised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-afzal-khan">Mohammad Afzal Khan</a> and urged a turn to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>; Sher Ali Khan expelled him in 1868. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> he joined the Council of Education and spoke at the opening of Istanbul University, but conservative clerics found his views too radical and he was expelled in 1871. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> he met the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abduh">Muhammad Abduh</a>, preached political reform, and sought the removal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isma-il-pasha">Isma'il Pasha</a>. He was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in August 1879.</p><p>In 1884 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abduh">Abduh</a> published the Arabic newspaper al-Urwah al-Wuthqa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, calling for a return to the principles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and for unity among Muslims. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in 1889, took sanctuary in the Shah Abdol-Azim Shrine, and was expelled in 1891. His agitation is thought to be one source of the Tobacco Protest of 1891. Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-hamid-ii">Abdul Hamid II</a> then invited him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>.</p><p>He joined a critique of Western imperialism to an appeal for Muslim unity, urging the adoption of Western science and institutions, and held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was compatible with reason. He wanted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and Muslims to cooperate against British rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. In 1881 he published Refutation of the Materialists, which included an early Islamic argument against Darwin; in later work he accepted evolution for animals but rejected it for humans, because humans have souls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> in 1895 he met his former disciple <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirza-reza-kermani">Mirza Reza Kermani</a>, and the two planned the assassination of Naser al-Din Shah. Kermani shot the Shah on 1 May 1896 and was hanged in August 1897. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> failed to obtain al-Afghani's extradition. He died of cancer of the jaw in Istanbul on 9 March 1897. In 1944 his remains were taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and laid in a mausoleum at Kabul University.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jamal al-Din al-Afghani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Jamal al-Din al-Afghani</b> and Muhammad Abduh argued that Islam, rightly understood, was a religion of reason, compatible with modern science, and called on Muslims to return to the principles of their faith and to exercise independent judgement, ijtihad, instead of following later authorities without question.</blockquote><a href="/movement/nahda">Nahda · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>At the end of the 19th century, as European colonial powers dismembered most of the Ottoman Empire, <b>Jamal al-Din al-Afghani</b> (1837–97), Muhammad Abduh (1849–1905) and Rashid Rida (1865–1935) preached Islamic alternatives to that decline.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/islamism">Islamism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jamal al-Din al-Afghani</b> feared that nationalism would divide the Muslim world and held that Muslim unity mattered more than ethnic identity.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pan-islamism">Pan-Islamism · Core Ideas</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Asadabad, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.87,71.15&amp;year=1246" aria-label="Asadabad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6355 902 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jamal al-Din al-Afghani"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6805 1172h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6805 1172h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.87,71.15&amp;year=1246">What was here in 1246? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
